# Copy to .env.local for bare-metal runs: `npm start` and `npm run dev` load WRITE_DATA_DIR,
# WRITE_CONFIG_DIR and WRITE_AUTH from it. With Docker Compose, set them under `environment:` in
# docker-compose.yml instead.

# Where your notes live, as plain .md files. Relative paths resolve from the directory you start
# the server in. Default: ./data (bare metal), /data (Docker).
# WRITE_DATA_DIR=./data

# Where write keeps your account (account.json) and its own settings: the AI assistant's connections
# (settings.json) and your integrations (integrations.json), API keys in plain text. Keep it outside
# WRITE_DATA_DIR, so a synced notes folder never carries your keys: write refuses a folder it can see
# is inside WRITE_DATA_DIR (symlinks followed), but in Docker two mounts of the same host folder look
# separate, so keep those apart yourself.
# Relative paths resolve like WRITE_DATA_DIR. Default: ./config (bare metal), /config (Docker).
# WRITE_CONFIG_DIR=./config

# Sign-in is on by default: the first time you open write it asks you to create an account, stored
# in WRITE_CONFIG_DIR/account.json. Set this to `off` only when something in front of write already
# signs people in (a VPN, or a reverse proxy with its own sign-in). Any other value leaves it on.
# WRITE_AUTH=off

# The port and bind address CAN'T be set in this file: `npm start` picks them before it reads .env
# files, so PORT or HOSTNAME here are silently ignored. Pass flags instead:
#   npm start -- -p 8080 -H 127.0.0.1
# PORT=8080 also works as a real environment variable (shell, systemd). `npm start` ignores
# HOSTNAME even there; it only applies to the Docker image.
